<ArticleLayout slug="raw-material-processing-cereals">
  <p>
    Processing cereals and protein crops by extrusion cooking offers definite
    advantages. Here they are in detail.
  </p>

  <h2>Starch gelatinisation</h2>
  <p>
    It improves the digestibility of the carbohydrates in the raw materials.
    Using a <a href="/en/solutions/preconditioners">preconditioner</a> with
    the addition of water and steam makes this gelatinisation easier and
    considerably increases the output of the extruder. Extrusion processing
    improves the efficiency of carbohydrates in young animals, or in species
    where amylase production is poorly developed.
  </p>

  <h2>Reduced protein solubility</h2>
  <p>
    The very short time during which the product to be extruded is subjected
    to very high temperatures does not cause any drop in protein
    digestibility due to the
    <a href="/en/expert-insights/maillard-reactions">Maillard reaction</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Extrusion causes the insolubilisation of the native proteins contained in
    protein crops and oilseeds. This transformation is particularly valuable
    for the use of these raw materials in ruminant feed, since it lowers the
    proportion of soluble nitrogen in these ingredients (PDIN) and increases
    the content of nitrogen that is not degradable in the rumen (PDIA).
  </p>

  <h2>The destruction of antinutritional factors</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      90% of the antitrypsic factors contained in soybean and protein crops
      are destroyed - see
      <a href="/en/expert-insights/soybean-processing-method">
        processing soybeans by extrusion</a>,
    </li>
    <li>
      the glucosinolate content of rapeseed and rapeseed meal, the gossypol
      in cotton and the aflatoxins in groundnut meal and in ingredients
      contaminated by these moulds are reduced,
    </li>
    <li>
      the enzyme systems responsible for the release of hydrocyanic acid from
      linseed and linseed meal are inactivated.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fat released and stabilised</h2>
  <p>
    Extrusion inactivates the lipases and lipoxidases present in oilseeds,
    which prevents the rancidity and oxidation of the oil they contain. It
    also releases the fat contained in the cells:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>increased metabolisable energy,</li>
    <li>stabilisation of the fat,</li>
    <li>reduced oxidation of the oil.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Forming the product</h2>
  <p>
    Passing through dies at the outlet of the extruder gives products of
    widely varied sizes and shapes.
  </p>

